 Part 1 – Assessing the damage and preparing the chair
In part 1 of this workshop guide, we will cover the initial steps of repairing a wooden chair. We will assess the damage, and prepare the chair for the repair process.
Repairing a wooden chair can be a rewarding DIY project, but it can also be a challenging one. The process requires patience, skill, and attention to detail. In this workshop guide, we will walk you through each step of the repair process, from assessing the damage to applying the final finish.
Assessing the Damage:
Before you begin the repair process, it is essential to assess the damage to the chair. This step will help you determine the extent of the damage and plan your repair strategy.
Step 1: Inspect the Chair
Carefully inspect the chair for any signs of damage, wear, or decay. Look for cracks, splits, or breaks in the wood, as well as any signs of insect infestation or rot. Take note of any loose joints, broken legs, or damaged seats.
Step 2: Identify the Type of Damage
Once you have inspected the chair, identify the type of damage that it has sustained. Is the damage due to wear and tear, a accident, or insect infestation? Knowing the cause of the damage will help you plan your repair strategy.
Preparing the Chair:
Once you have assessed the damage, it is time to prepare the chair for the repair process. This step is crucial, as it will help ensure a successful and durable repair.

Step 1: Remove the Seat and Back
If the seat or back of the chair is damaged, remove it to make the repair process easier. This will also give you better access to the legs and joints of the chair.

Step 2: Clean the Chair
Use a soft-bristled brush or a vacuum cleaner to remove any dirt, dust, or debris from the chair. This will help prevent any further damage and make the repair process